+# Decision: Preview cache eviction policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private cache of rendered JPEG contact sheets bounded at 12 GiB on one eight-core machine during the pilot. Editors alternate two access shapes against this cache: long sequential screenings that touch many clips once, and repeated comparisons of a small working set of clips. The mix varies by production. When the cache is full, the worker must choose which sheet to evict to admit a newly rendered one, and that choice decides whether a long one-pass screening displaces the working set an editor is actively comparing.
+
+Every candidate below renders the same sheets, honors the same 12 GiB budget on one worker, keeps all replacement state private to the worker, and serves the same ready/pending/unknown contract regardless of policy. Those are fixed invariants, so they do not discriminate between the options; what follows are the considerations that do.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scan resistance for the comparison working set** *(major)* — how well the policy keeps a small, repeatedly-accessed working set resident while a long sequential screening streams many one-use sheets through the cache. This is the workload's defining tension and the criterion the choice turns on: the design leans toward retaining the reused comparison set over one-pass screening sheets.
+- **Adaptation when an editor switches projects** *(moderate)* — how quickly the resident set follows a shift to a new working set, since the mix and subject change by production.
+- **Bookkeeping and maintenance cost** *(moderate)* — per-access and background work the policy adds on one eight-core worker serving 24 editors.
+- **Tuning burden** *(minor)* — how many parameters must be set and maintained for the policy to behave well.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description.** Evict the least-recently-used resident sheet. A single recency list; each access moves its entry to the front, eviction takes the tail.
+
+- **Scan resistance:** weak. A screening longer than the cache walks the entire working set to the tail and evicts it, because every scanned sheet looks as recent as a compared one.
+- **Adaptation:** strong. The resident set is exactly the most recent accesses, so a project switch is followed within one working set's worth of requests.
+- **Bookkeeping:** strong. One list, one move per access, no background work.
+- **Tuning:** strong. No parameters beyond the byte budget.
+
+**Summary.** Cheapest and most responsive, but a long screening evicts the comparison set.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description.** Evict the sheet with the lowest access-frequency count, where counts decay over time so that once-popular sheets eventually fall out. Each entry carries a counter; a background or amortized aging pass decays counts.
+
+- **Scan resistance:** partial. One-use scan sheets accumulate low counts and are preferred for eviction, protecting repeatedly-viewed sheets — but a sheet must be accessed enough times to build a defensible count before it is protected.
+- **Adaptation:** weak. Yesterday's high counts keep a previous project's working set resident until aging decays it, lagging a switch.
+- **Bookkeeping:** weak. Per-entry counters plus a recurring aging pass add sustained work on the single worker.
+- **Tuning:** partial. The aging rate and decay shape must be chosen and maintained.
+
+**Summary.** Best retention of durably-popular sheets, at the cost of counter upkeep and slow adaptation.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description.** Separate first-use entries from reused entries. A newly rendered sheet enters a FIFO first-use list (A1in). Only a sheet accessed a second time — recognized by a ghost list (A1out) of recently evicted first-use keys — is admitted to an LRU reused list (Am). A sheet earns a place in the protected reused set only by being accessed again after a first-use eviction, so a one-pass scan never reaches it.
+
+- **Scan resistance:** strong. First-use scan sheets flow through A1in and out without ever entering Am, so a screening cannot evict the reused working set.
+- **Adaptation:** partial. A new project's sheets enter as first-use and are promoted to the reused set on their second access, a short lag behind pure recency.
+- **Bookkeeping:** partial. Two resident lists plus a byte-free ghost list and per-access list maintenance — more than LRU, but no counters and no background aging pass.
+- **Tuning:** partial. Two parameters: the budget share for the first-use list and the ghost-list size.
+
+**Summary.** Protects the reused comparison set from screening scans, at the cost of a second queue, a ghost list, and two size parameters.
+
+## Selection
+
+**2Q.** The major criterion is scan resistance for the comparison working set, and 2Q is the only option that fits it strongly: admitting a sheet to the protected reused set requires a second access, which a one-pass screening never produces, so a long screening cannot displace the set an editor is comparing. LRU fails exactly this case, and aged frequency protects popular sheets only after they accrue counts and then clings to a previous project's set. The costs 2Q carries fall on the moderate and minor criteria the pilot accepts: more per-access bookkeeping than LRU (but no counters or aging pass), a short promotion lag on a project switch, and two size parameters (the first-use budget share and the ghost-list size) to tune.
+
+The decision turns on the lean toward **retaining the reused comparison set over one-pass screening sheets**. Committing to 2Q obligates the worker to maintain the two resident lists and the ghost list as private bookkeeping, and obligates tuning of the two size parameters per the deferred open question in the LLD. It forecloses nothing in the external contract, the budget, or the single-worker deployment, all of which are identical across the options.
