+## Eviction: 2Q
+
+The cache admits sheets through a 2Q replacement policy, which separates first-use entries from reused entries so a long sequential screening cannot evict the smaller working set an editor is comparing against. Three structures hold this split; all are private worker bookkeeping, and none changes the external interface, render-queue concurrency, or the 12 GiB disk budget.
+
+- **A1in** — a FIFO list of first-use resident sheets. A newly rendered sheet whose key is not remembered in A1out is admitted here. A hit on an A1in entry is served from it but does not move the entry; recency alone does not promote a first-use sheet.
+- **Am** — an LRU list of reused resident sheets. A sheet is admitted here only when its key is found in A1out at render time. A hit on an Am entry moves it to the Am most-recently-used position.
+- **A1out** — a ghost list of recently evicted A1in keys, holding clip ID, revision, and renderer version only — no JPEG bytes. Its purpose is to recognize a second access to a sheet that a scan already pushed out of A1in, which is what admits that sheet to Am.
+
+Resident JPEG bytes in A1in and Am together stay within the 12 GiB budget. The budget is split by a tunable share **Kin**: A1in holds up to Kin of the budget, Am holds the remainder. A1out holds up to a tunable count **Kout** of ghost keys and is bounded by key count, not bytes, since it stores no image data.
+
+**Admission and eviction.** When a completed sheet is admitted, if its key is present in A1out, the sheet enters Am (as MRU) and that ghost key is removed; otherwise it enters A1in (as the FIFO head). Before admission, the worker evicts to stay within 12 GiB: while A1in's resident bytes exceed its Kin share, it evicts A1in's FIFO tail, recording that tail's key in A1out (dropping A1out's oldest ghost key if A1out is at Kout); otherwise it evicts Am's LRU tail, which is forgotten entirely and leaves no ghost key.
+
+The cache key remains clip ID, revision, and renderer version, as in rendering and deduplication above. Because keys are immutable, a ghost key in A1out always refers to the same source; a superseded revision or a new renderer version is simply a different key and ages out of A1out by count.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (A1in FIFO, Am LRU, A1out ghost keys) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparison of a small working set; 2Q admits a sheet to the reused queue only on a second access, so a one-pass screening scan cannot evict the comparison set. LRU is cheaper but a long scan evicts the working set; aged frequency counts protect popular clips but add counter maintenance and lag a project switch. The accepted cost is two resident queues plus a ghost list and the Kin/Kout tuning parameters. Full options and criteria: [decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md](decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md). |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Initial values for Kin (A1in's share of the 12 GiB budget) and Kout (ghost-key count), and whether they are tuned per production. The policy is correct for any split; the values set how aggressively first-use sheets are protected.
